{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>These are the generations of Noah — Noah was a righteous man.</b> Since Scripture mentions him, it recounts his praise, as it says: ״זֵכֶר צַדִּיק לִבְרָכָה״ (Proverbs 10). Another thing: it teaches you that the principal generations of the righteous are good deeds." "<b>In his generations.</b> Some of our Sages interpret it for praise — all the more so had he been in a generation of the righteous, he would have been more righteous. And some interpret it for disgrace — in relation to his generation he was righteous, but had he been in the generation of Abraham he would have been accounted as nothing (Sanhedrin 108)." "<b>Noah walked with Elohim.</b> But of Abraham He says ״אֲשֶׁר הִתְהַלַּכְתִּי לְפָנָיו״ (Genesis 24)? Noah needed support to uphold him, but Abraham grew strong and walked in his righteousness of himself." "<b>Walked.</b> Past tense — and this is the usage of the root: in the heavy (binyan) form it serves both for the future and for the past, but in the kal form, ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ״ (ibid. 13) is future; ״הִתְהַלֶּךְ נֹחַ״ is past. ״הִתְפַּלֵּל בְּעַד עֲבָדֶיךָ״ (I Samuel 12) is future; ״וּבֹא וְהִתְפַּלֵּל אֶל הַבַּיִת הַזֶּה״ (I Kings 8) is past tense — except that the vav at its beginning converted it to the future."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:57:45.803755189Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}